<h2></h2>
<h2>String Bending</h2>
<p>Bends are a great way to add interest to your playing. &nbsp;Bends&nbsp;=
raise the pitch of the note. &nbsp;The amount of pitch change depends on th=
e distance you bend. &nbsp;Usually, you want to change the pitch by a half =
step or a whole step. &nbsp;Sometimes you want to change it even more, but =
you=E2=80=99ll need some good calluses on your fingers for that!</p>
<h3>Executing a Bend</h3>
<ol>
<li>Fret the first note.</li>
<li>Pluck the string.</li>
<li>Without raising your finger off the fretboard, slide it vertically up o=
r down (parallel to the fret).&nbsp; Bend until you hit the target pitch, w=
hich is the second note.</li>
</ol>
<p><span class=3D"info">When notating bends in tablature, it usually indica=
tes how many steps to bend the note (example: 1/2, full, 1 1/2).&nbsp; Sinc=
e each 1/2 step is equal to one fret, you can test to make sure you=E2=80=
=99re bending the proper amount. For 1/2 step bends, bend the note until it=
=E2=80=99s the same pitch as next fret (ex: Bending the 10th fret bent a 1/=
2 step is the same pitch as the 11th fret).&nbsp; For whole step bends (lab=
eled full in tablature), bend the note until it=E2=80=99s the same pitch as=
 the note that is two frets up.</span></p>
